  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Free personal care for older people; recruit 1,500 nurses; halve inpatient waits; reintroduce free dental check-ups; freeze/review prescription charges; national anti-poverty strategy; strengthen disability rights; abolish tuition fees and rebuild maintenance grants; lower class sizes; tackle homelessness; concessionary travel for youth/older/disabled; fast and fair asylum decisions (oppose vouchers); 1,000 additional police; lifetime supervision for high-risk violent/sexual offenders; Sentencing Advisory Panel; proportional representation and inclusive citizenship; raise the minimum wage and strengthen trade union rights. TEXT_F: Increase police numbers; tougher sentences (e.g., mandatory prison for selling drugs to under‑16s, end automatic early release); lifetime supervision for sex offenders; easier exclusion for disruptive pupils; secure reception centres for asylum; Patient’s Guarantee on waiting times with choice of NHS/private; Exceptional Medicines Fund; increased basic state pension (larger uplift for over‑75s); outcome‑based ‘can work, must work’ welfare; prison work with proceeds to victims; broad tax cuts and deregulation.",
  "reasoning": "Because care and authority are the top priorities in the profile, TEXT_G’s extensive, concrete care policies (health, poverty, education, disability, homelessness) alongside meaningful authority measures (more police, tighter supervision and sentencing structures) better match the weighting than TEXT_F’s authority‑heavy but comparatively thinner care offer. TEXT_G also aligns more with the profile’s mid‑level equality priority, whereas TEXT_F leans more toward proportionality and market measures than equality.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
